{"id":"neet-2024-q161","pn":161,"y":"NEET 2024 · 5 May","tp":"Digestion and Absorption","st":"Digestive enzymes and bonds","q":"Match List I with List II :\nA. Lipase — I. Peptide bond\nB. Nuclease — II. Ester bond\nC. Protease — III. Glycosidic bond\nD. Amylase — IV. Phosphodiester bond","o":["A-II, B-IV, C-I, D-III","A-IV, B-I, C-III, D-II","A-IV, B-II, C-III, D-I","A-III, B-II, C-I, D-IV"],"c":0,"s":"Option (1) is correct.\nA. Lipase digests the ester bonds of lipids.\nB. Nuclease digests the phosphodiester bonds of nucleic acids.\nC. Protease digests the peptide bonds of proteins.\nD. Amylase breaks the glycosidic bonds of carbohydrates.\nIt turns starch into smaller molecules and finally maltose, which maltase splits into two glucose molecules."},
{"id":"neet-2023-q156","pn":156,"y":"NEET 2023 · 7 May","tp":"Digestion and Absorption","st":"Gastric gland cells","q":"Match List I with List II.\nList I (Cells): A. Peptic cells; B. Goblet cells; C. Oxyntic cells; D. Hepatic cells\nList II (Secretion): I. Mucus; II. Bile Juice; III. Proenzyme pepsinogen; IV. HCl and intrinsic factor for absorption of vitamin B12.\nChoose the correct answer from the options given below :","o":["A-II, B-IV, C-I, D-III","A-IV, B-III, C-II, D-I","A-II, B-I, C-III, D-IV","A-III, B-I, C-IV, D-II"],"c":3,"s":"Gastric glands have three main cell types.\nMucus neck cells (goblet cells) secrete mucus.\nPeptic (chief) cells secrete the proenzyme pepsinogen.\nOxyntic (parietal) cells secrete HCl and intrinsic factor, needed for vitamin B12 absorption.\nHepatic cells make bile juice."},
{"id":"neet-2023-q172","pn":172,"y":"NEET 2023 · 7 May","tp":"Digestion and Absorption","st":"Large intestine and sphincters","q":"Once the undigested and unabsorbed substances enter the caecum, their backflow is prevented by","o":["Pyloric sphincter","Sphincter of Oddi","Ileo-caecal valve","Gastro-oesophageal sphincter"],"c":2,"s":"Undigested food passes into the caecum through the ileo-caecal valve, which stops it flowing back.\nGastro-oesophageal sphincter guards the oesophagus-stomach opening.\nPyloric sphincter guards the stomach-duodenum opening.\nSphincter of Oddi guards the common hepato-pancreatic duct opening."},
{"id":"neet-2022r-q174","pn":174,"y":"Re-NEET 2022 · 4 Sep","tp":"Digestion and Absorption","st":"Structure of tooth","q":"Role of enamel is to :","o":["Give basic shape to the teeth","Connect crown of tooth with its root","Masticate the food","Form bolus"],"c":2,"s":"Enamel covers the outer side of the crown of the tooth.\nIt helps in chewing (mastication) of food."},
{"id":"neet-2022-q175","pn":175,"y":"NEET 2022 · 17 Jul","tp":"Digestion and Absorption","st":"Absorption of fats","q":"Given below are two statements :\nStatement I : Fatty acids and glycerols cannot be absorbed into the blood.\nStatement II : Specialized lymphatic capillaries called lacteals carry chylomicrons into lymphatic vessels and ultimately into the blood.\nIn the light of the above statements, choose the most appropriate answer from the options given below:","o":["Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect","Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect","Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct","Both Statement I and Statement II are correct"],"c":3,"s":"Fatty acids and glycerol are insoluble in water, so they cannot be absorbed directly into the blood.\nThey first form micelles that move into the intestinal mucosa.\nThere they are rebuilt into protein-coated fat globules called chylomicrons.\nChylomicrons enter the lacteals (lymph vessels) in the villi, which finally release them into the blood.\nSo both statements are correct."},
{"id":"neet-2022r-q175","pn":175,"y":"Re-NEET 2022 · 4 Sep","tp":"Digestion and Absorption","st":"Digestive enzymes and substrates","q":"Choose the incorrect enzymatic reaction.","o":["Dipeptides →(Dipeptidases) Amino acids","Maltose →(Maltase) Glucose + Galactose","Sucrose →(Sucrase) Glucose + Fructose","Lactose →(Lactase) Glucose + Galactose"],"c":1,"s":"Maltase splits maltose into two glucose molecules, not glucose and galactose.\nDipeptidase splits dipeptides into amino acids.\nSucrase splits sucrose into glucose and fructose.\nLactase splits lactose into glucose and galactose."},
{"id":"neet-2022-q183","pn":183,"y":"NEET 2022 · 17 Jul","tp":"Digestion and Absorption","st":"Salivary digestion","q":"Which of the following functions in not performed by secretions from salivary glands?","o":["Digestion of complex carbohydrates","Lubrication of oral cavity","Digestion of disaccharides","Control bacterial population in mouth"],"c":2,"s":"Saliva digests starch (a complex carbohydrate), so (1) is performed.\nIts mucus lubricates the mouth, so (2) is performed.\nIts lysozyme checks bacteria, so (4) is performed.\nDisaccharides are digested in the small intestine, not by saliva, so (3) is the answer."},
{"id":"neet-2021-q153","pn":153,"y":"NEET 2021 · 12 Sep","tp":"Digestion and Absorption","st":"Digestive juices","q":"Succus entericus is referred to as:","o":["Chyme","Pancreatic juice","Intestinal juice","Gastric juice"],"c":2,"s":"Succus entericus is the intestinal juice secreted by the small intestine.\nChyme is the acidic food in the stomach, pancreatic juice comes from the pancreas and gastric juice from the stomach."},
{"id":"neet-2021-q175","pn":175,"y":"NEET 2021 · 12 Sep","tp":"Digestion and Absorption","st":"Sphincter of Oddi","q":"Sphincter of oddi is present at:","o":["Junction of jejunum and duodenum","Ileo-caecal junction","Junction of hepato-pancreatic duct and duodenum","Gastro-oesophageal junction"],"c":2,"s":"The bile duct and pancreatic duct join as the hepato-pancreatic duct, which opens into the duodenum.\nThe sphincter of Oddi guards this opening."},
{"id":"neet-2020-q052","pn":52,"y":"NEET 2020 · 13 Sep","tp":"Digestion and Absorption","st":"Digestive enzymes","q":"The enzyme enterokinase helps in conversion of :","o":["trypsinogen into trypsin","caseinogen into pepsin","pepsinogen into pepsin","protein into polypeptides"],"c":0,"s":"Enterokinase of the intestinal juice activates trypsinogen into trypsin."},
{"id":"neet-2020-q053","pn":53,"y":"NEET 2020 · 13 Sep","tp":"Digestion and Absorption","st":"Alimentary canal","q":"Identify the correct statement with reference to human digestive system.","o":["Serosa is the innermost layer of the alimentary canal.","Ileum is a highly coiled part.","Vermiform appendix arises from duodenum.","Ileum opens into small intestine"],"c":1,"s":"Ileum is the highly coiled last part of the small intestine.\nSerosa is the outermost layer; the appendix arises from the caecum; the ileum opens into the large intestine."},
{"id":"neet-2019-q017","pn":17,"y":"NEET 2019 · 5 May","tp":"Digestion and Absorption","st":"Digestive glands","q":"Match the following structures with their respective location in organs :\n(a) Crypts of Lieberkuhn (i) Pancreas\n(b) Glisson’s capsule (ii) Duodenum\n(c) Islets of Langerhans (iii) Small intestine\n(d) Brunner’s Glands (iv) Liver\nSelect the correct option.","o":["(a)-(iii), (b)-(iv), (c)-(i), (d)-(ii)","(a)-(iii), (b)-(ii), (c)-(i), (d)-(iv)","(a)-(iii), (b)-(i), (c)-(ii), (d)-(iv)","(a)-(ii), (b)-(iv), (c)-(i), (d)-(iii)"],"c":0,"s":"Crypts of Lieberkuhn are in the small intestine.\nGlisson’s capsule covers the liver.\nIslets of Langerhans are in the pancreas.\nBrunner’s glands are in the submucosa of the duodenum."},
{"id":"neet-2019-q024","pn":24,"y":"NEET 2019 · 5 May","tp":"Digestion and Absorption","st":"Gastric glands","q":"Identify the cells whose secretion protects the lining of gastro-intestinal tract from various enzymes.","o":["Oxyntic Cells","Duodenal Cells","Chief Cells","Goblet Cells"],"c":3,"s":"Goblet cells (mucous cells) secrete mucus.\nMucus coats the lining and protects it from enzymes and acid."},
{"id":"neet-2018-q150","pn":150,"y":"NEET 2018 · 6 May","tp":"Digestion and Absorption","st":"Human dentition","q":"Which of the following terms describe human dentition ?","o":["Thecodont, Diphyodont, Homodont.","Thecodont, Diphyodont, Heterodont.","Pleurodont, Monophyodont, Homodont.","Pleurodont, Diphyodont, Heterodont."],"c":1,"s":"Human teeth are set in sockets in the jaw bone: thecodont.\nTwo sets appear in life, milk teeth and permanent teeth: diphyodont.\nThere are incisors, canines, premolars and molars: heterodont."},
{"id":"neet-2018-q177","pn":177,"y":"NEET 2018 · 6 May","tp":"Digestion and Absorption","st":"Gastric cells","q":"Which of the following gastric cells indirectly help in erythropoiesis?","o":["Chief cells","Mucous cells","Goblet cells","Parietal cells"],"c":3,"s":"Parietal (oxyntic) cells secrete HCl and the intrinsic factor.\nIntrinsic factor is needed to absorb vitamin B₁₂, which red blood cell formation needs.\nSo parietal cells help erythropoiesis indirectly."},
{"id":"neet-2017-q067","pn":67,"y":"NEET 2017 · 7 May","tp":"Digestion and Absorption","st":"Pancreatic juice","q":"Which of the following options best represents the enzyme composition of pancreatic juice ?","o":["Lipase, amylase, trypsinogen, procarboxypeptidase","Amylase, peptidase, trypsinogen, rennin","Amylase, pepsin, trypsinogen, maltase","Peptidase, amylase, pepsin, rennin"],"c":0,"s":"Pancreatic juice contains inactive trypsinogen, chymotrypsinogen, procarboxypeptidase, and also amylase, lipase and nucleases.\nPepsin and rennin come from the stomach and maltase from the intestinal juice."},
{"id":"neet-2017-q117","pn":117,"y":"NEET 2017 · 7 May","tp":"Digestion and Absorption","st":"Human dentition","q":"A baby boy aged two years is admitted to play school and passes through a dental check-up. The dentist observed that the boy had twenty teeth. Which teeth were absent ?","o":["Molars","Incisors","Canines","Pre-molars"],"c":3,"s":"The milk (deciduous) dental formula is I 2/2, C 1/1, PM 0/0, M 2/2 on each side, giving 20 teeth.\nSo pre-molars are absent in milk teeth."},
{"id":"neet-2017-q120","pn":120,"y":"NEET 2017 · 7 May","tp":"Digestion and Absorption","st":"Intestinal glands","q":"Which cells of ‘Crypts of Lieberkuhn’ secrete antibacterial lysozyme ?","o":["Kupffer cells","Argentaffin cells","Paneth cells","Zymogen cells"],"c":2,"s":"Paneth cells in the crypts of Lieberkuhn secrete lysozyme, which kills bacteria.\nKupffer cells are in the liver and zymogen cells are in the stomach."},
{"id":"neet-2016-q112","pn":112,"y":"AIPMT 2016 · 1 May","tp":"Digestion and Absorption","st":"Sphincter of Oddi","q":"Which of the following guards the opening of hepatopancreatic duct into the duodenum?","o":["Ileocaecal valve","Pyloric sphincter","Sphincter of Oddi","Semilunar valve"],"c":2,"s":"The common bile duct and pancreatic duct join as the hepatopancreatic duct and open into the duodenum.\nThis opening is guarded by the sphincter of Oddi."},
{"id":"neet-2016-q113","pn":113,"y":"AIPMT 2016 · 1 May","tp":"Digestion and Absorption","st":"Gastric glands","q":"In the stomach, gastric acid is secreted by the :","o":["Parietal cells","Peptic cells","Acidic cells","Gastrin secreting cells"],"c":0,"s":"Parietal (oxyntic) cells of the gastric glands secrete HCl and intrinsic factor.\nPeptic (chief) cells secrete pepsinogen."},
{"id":"neet-2016b-q147","pn":147,"y":"AIPMT 2016 · 24 Jul","tp":"Digestion and Absorption","st":"Hormonal control of digestion","q":"Which hormones do stimulate the production of pancreatic juice and bicarbonate ?","o":["Angiotensin and epinephrine","Gastrin and insulin","Cholecystokinin and secretin","Insulin and glucagon"],"c":2,"s":"Secretin acts on the pancreas to release water and bicarbonate.\nCholecystokinin (CCK) stimulates the pancreas to release digestive enzymes and the gall bladder to release bile.\nSo cholecystokinin and secretin together stimulate pancreatic juice."}
